gitree (0.3.4-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * The commit list shows the short hash of each commit, in a column to
    the left of the author. A search marks the part of the hash that
    matches.
  * A right-click on a hash opens a menu that copies the full hash. A
    right-click on a ref label opens a menu that copies the name of the
    ref. The right-click does not change the selection.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:11:00 +0200

gitree (0.3.3-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * A click on a commit shows its details and its diff. Before, only a
    double click did this. A click on the same commit hides them again.
    Each click counts, thus a double click shows the pane and then hides
    it.
  * Enter continues to fill the window with the diff when the commit
    changes only one file. A click does not.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:53:31 +0200

gitree (0.3.2-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * A tick or an untick on the left does not move the list. The commit
    at the top of the list stays at the top. If that commit goes, the
    next commit below it takes its position. Before, the list went back
    to the first row. A change to a history setting also keeps the list
    in position.
  * At the first row, the list stays at the first row. Thus a tick of a
    branch with newer commits shows these commits. Before, they stayed
    above the visible rows.
  * A reload keeps the same commit at the top of the list. Before, a
    reload kept only the distance from the top, thus each new commit
    moved the rows down.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:15:00 +0200

gitree (0.3.1-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * A click on a commit shows the row as selected at once. Before, the
    row changed colour up to 0.4 s later, because gitree made the diff
    first.
  * The diff of a commit with two or more files shows sooner. A file
    makes its text only when you open it. On a commit with many files,
    the first files show at once and the rest follow.
  * A diff with a Markdown file shows about 0.2 s sooner. gitree keeps
    the syntax rules of each file type loaded, so it does not load them
    again for each diff.
  * In the unified view, an added line can come after a removed line
    with no newline at its end. The changed words on the added line now
    have their mark. Before, the mark went to the next line.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 13:50:00 +0200

gitree (0.3.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * While the diff fills the window, a close button shows at its top
    right. A click on it brings back the refs and the list, and the diff
    stays open.
  * A double click or Enter on a commit that changes one file fills the
    window with the diff at once.
  * A click that closes a file, or a click on Collapse all, does not fill
    the window. In 0.2.0 it did.
  * A file in the diff closes at once when you click it. Before, its text
    stayed on the screen for a quarter of a second.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 16:35:16 +0200

gitree (0.2.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * The refs on the left follow the order of the graph. A ref whose
    commit is higher in the history comes first, and a group takes the
    place of its highest ref.
  * The details pane opens below the refs and the list, and it is as wide
    as the window.
  * A click on a file, or on Expand all, fills the window with the diff.
    Escape or the back arrow brings back the refs and the list.
  * Escape closes the details also when no widget has the focus, for
    example after the divider is dragged to the top.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 15:12:13 +0200

gitree (0.1.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Initial release. gitree shows the history of the branches, remote
    branches and tags that you tick, drawn the way gitg draws it. Names
    with slashes fold into groups, a double click on a commit shows its
    details and its diff, and a path keeps only the commits that change a
    file or a folder. The command is gitree, and git tree runs it too.

 -- alexandros filotheou <alexandros.filotheou@gmail.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 12:37:45 +0200
